.he "who (1)                      9/30/26                      who (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
who
- who is logged in
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
who
.br
.bd
who am i
.br
.bd
who file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Who
prints a list of the currently logged-in users of Micronix.  One line
is printed for each login still recorded in the file, giving the user
name, the terminal and the time of the login, in that order.
.sp
A record whose name is empty is skipped, because an empty name is what a
terminal's record holds once it has been logged out of and not yet
logged into again.  An empty record is only decoration; only records
with a name are logins.
.sp
The name is printed in eight columns and the terminal in eight columns,
both truncated to the eight bytes the file holds and padded where they
are shorter.  The time comes from the record and is printed as the month
and day, the hour and the minute, up to twelve characters; the year and
the seconds are not printed.
.sp
.bd
who am i
answers for one login instead of listing them: the one on the terminal
who was run from, found by matching the terminal's name against the
records.  Run from a terminal that is not in the file, or with no
terminal at all, it falls back on the login name and prints a
.bd
tty??
line for it.
.sp
.bd
who file
reads its records from file rather than from /etc/utmp.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The 1982 page gives the command as
.bd
who
with no arguments and says only that it prints a list of the currently
logged-in users, which is the first form above.  The other two are what
the v7 source has and this port keeps.
.sp
The records are read twenty bytes at a time, so a file that is not a
utmp file is read as though it were one: the bytes are taken as a name,
a line and a time whether or not that is what they are.
.sp
Who has to know which terminal it is running on, and there is no
ttyname in this tree's library.  It finds its own by reading the /dev
directory a name at a time and asking the system about each one, until
one has the device and the inode that the system reports for its own
terminal - which is how login does it too, and is why
.bd
who am i
is the slow form of who.
.sp
The exit status is 0 after a listing, which is not what the original
did: v7's who left main by falling off the end of it, and a status that
is whatever the return register happened to hold is no use to a shell.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/utmp	(current user information)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
